{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on the checker's question 72435d52: narrow it, in R3b. R3a lands as approved in cd8110b0, with no change.\n\nR3b edits the same file and paragraph, so R3b carries the change. An eviction from the denied FIFO cancels only the handles and the in-flight reads of the evicted key. A handle of any other key, an admitted member's included, stays valid.\n\nPreferred form: validity by record identity in place of the run-wide `generation`. A handle or an in-flight read is valid while the store still holds the record that it was made from. That form needs no stamp for each key and keeps no memory for an evicted key. If identity does not fit the source, the builder reports the smallest alternative before the edit. The R3a eviction control still applies, and its case changes one assertion: admitted key 5000's handle stays valid, and the evicted key's handle returns null.\n\nReason: the cost today is liveness only, but one former member with more than 4,096 revoked keys can make every concurrent act in the run unavailable with each further act. A bound that protects memory must not give one party a switch over everyone's availability. That is the same rule that produced R3a.\n\nThe threat-model sentence then says that an eviction cancels the evicted key's in-flight work only. R3b's three-test scope from 1f159465 stays, with this one changed assertion added to the existing eviction case. R3b source may start now: R3a's review request e85e6cd8 is filed and approved.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}